00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
when we were chatting a few months ago i  said that hard times were coming  and today's numbers show that hard times  are here  hundreds of thousands of people have  already lost their job  and many more will what i would say  though is  difficult decisions do lie ahead for all  of us  but i want to reassure people that we  will get through this and nobody  will be left without hope and  opportunity why do you think it is that  the uk has been so badly hit i mean  you know the deepest recession of pretty  much any g7 economy yeah i think that's  a it's a very fair question to ask and  as i look at it i think  the primary explanation is the  composition of our economy  social activities for example going out  for a meal going to the cinema shopping  those kinds of things comprise a much  larger share of our economy than they do  for most of our  european comparative countries so in a  situation where you've literally  shut down all those industries for  almost three months for a long period of  time  it is unfortunately going to have an  outsized impact on our economy  and it's it's it's had a big impact on  the economy and as you say will likely  have an impact in the coming months i  mean  what can we expect in terms of you know  the economy in terms of people's jobs  well the employment picture as i've said  is  difficult hundreds of thousands of  people have already lost their jobs  many more will uh that is incredibly sad  and why it's so important that we focus  on trying to  help those people which is why we  outlined our plan for jobs  in july to support protect and create  jobs across the united kingdom  and whether it's through the job  retention bonus whether it's through  initiatives like eat out to help  out or the kickstart scheme for young  people or new apprenticeship incentives  i want to provide people with new  opportunities  one of the things that i think is in  everyone's mind at the moment is the  possibility of there being a second  surgeon  in cases across the uk if that does  happen  are you prepared potentially to shut  down parts of the economy again whether  that's pubs or restaurants and  you know the bits that have just  reopened right now the prime minister  said that might have to happen if  schools are going to reopen  i think the prime minister's been very  clear i think we're dealing with an  unprecedented pandemic it would be wrong  for me or anyone to sit here and say we  have a perfect crystal ball on  on how the future is is going to be but  obviously we we've evolved to a new  strategy now  which you know shows enormous promise  which is the ability to target our  interventions  in a much more nuanced way and that's  the success of our track and trace  operation  the testing capacity and so now we can  intervene at a local level  at an earlier stage and be much more  targeted in our approach and  you know so far that that is working and  that's very much how we plan to deal  with this going forward but if that  doesn't work you know that is still  early days if that doesn't work you're  prepared are you to do  bigger lockdowns across larger parts of  the economy are you  i think i think we've shown you can see  from our actions whether it's on  things like travel or indeed intervening  in local areas  in varying degrees that we will take the  action that's required  to suppress the spread of the virus and  do that in a way  that you know allows as much of the  economy to function as possible  you know that remains our our guiding  principle but as i said  i can't pretend that i have a crystal  ball given we're dealing with an  unprecedented situation and  and one of the things that's been  reported recently is the possibility you  know you may consider  changing uh the scope of the spending  review potentially  the the date of the the budget if there  is a second surge i mean  are those things that are possible  we've said that we plan to have a fiscal  event in the autumn and we're very much  working towards  that goal indeed on the spending review  which sets  all the budgets for people's departments  uh for the next few years i launched  that process just a few weeks ago by  writing to departments so you know we're  on track  for that but but you know it could be a  one year it could be a three-year  thing you know there's quite different  scopes in terms of how ambitious you're  going to be  and how much of a vision you can have  for the uk economy in the next few  months  well i think we're leaving at a time of  unprecedented uncertainty economically  and grappling with something that we  haven't done before so again i think we  you know we should have some humility  about the situation we're in  but our plan very much as i said is to  have a fiscal event in the autumn and  that's what we're working towards  you've probably seen the pictures over  the course of the last few  weeks of um dinghies in the channel of  people  trying to get to this country um and  you'll know about the government's  reaction to that what's your impression  when you see those pictures  well i share with with most people an  enormous sense of anger and frustration  that this is still happening this is  something that people rightly believe  um should be stopping france is a safe  country people should not be traveling  from france to come to the uk  for asylum purposes the immigration  minister has has been in france engaging  uh with our allies over there obviously  this is something that we have to tackle  jointly and he's working very hard  to put in place whatever measures are  required to do exactly that  finally when you when you look at the  numbers that we've just seen you know 24  20.4 in terms of the fall in gdp  was that just a price worth paying for  keeping the disease under control i  think we we took the decisions that we  did at the time we did in order to  protect people's lives and suppress the  spread of the  virus and those were the right decisions  but it's also  now right given the progress we've made  to  have safely reopened our economy it's  not a sustainable situation  to have vast swathes of our economy  essentially shut down  and that's why we have been able to  successfully reopen bits and do it in a  safe way and as people  get back to going shopping or going out  for a meal or indeed getting back to  their office they will see that it's a  new normal it's a safe normal  we have made changes guidance is there  to ensure that safety  and crucially mean that we can get our  lives back to normal get the economy  going again but do so in a way that  maintains our suppression of the virus  and they shouldn't be too nervous about  the virus should they  no i think as people will see as they go  out whether it's to a shop whether it's  to a restaurant whether it indeed  is back to their office i think they  will see changes and those changes are  quite  tangible and people should take  confidence from those changes to know  that this is now a new safer environment  for me to go and do all those things and  that's good news i think we need to get  the economy going we talked about jobs  earlier in this interview  and we want to try and protect as many  jobs as possible that comes from  as much of our economy returning to  normal as possible and that's why we're  so focused on these things
 

